		summary = "A **white dwarf** is a very [[Density|dense]] type of star: in an [[Earth]]-sized volume, it packs a mass that is comparable to the [[Sun]]. A white dwarf radiates light from residual [[Heat|heat]] and eventual [[Crystallization|crystallization]], not from [[Nuclear fusion|nuclear fusion]]. Stars like the Sun, whose [[Mass|mass]] is not high enough to collapse into a [[Neutron star|neutron star]] or [[Black hole|black hole]], are expected to become white dwarf stars later in their [[Stellar evolution|evolution]]. The nearest known white dwarf is Sirius B, at 8.6 light years, the smaller component of the Sirius [[Binary star|binary star]].",
